('t1-mcq-01', 1, '1.1.2', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'Which of the following is a positive economic statement?', null, '["A The government should raise the National Living Wage to reduce in-work poverty.","B Following a 10% rise in cinema ticket prices, attendance fell by 4% over the next year.","C Income inequality in the UK is unacceptably high.","D Sugary drinks ought to be taxed more heavily than they are now."]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-02', 1, '1.1.4', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'The table shows combinations of goods an economy can produce when all its resources are fully and efficiently employed (illustrative data).

Combination | Capital goods (units) | Consumer goods (units)
P | 0 | 100
Q | 10 | 90
R | 20 | 75
S | 30 | 55

What is the opportunity cost of each additional unit of capital goods when the economy moves from R to S?', null, '["A 2 units of consumer goods","B 0.5 units of consumer goods","C 20 units of consumer goods","D 55 units of consumer goods"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-03', 1, '1.1.5', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'A café chalks the prices of all its food and drinks on a board in pounds and pence so that customers can compare the cost of different items. Which function of money does this best illustrate?', null, '["A A medium of exchange","B A store of value","C A unit of account (measure of value)","D A method of deferred payment"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-04', 1, '1.1.6', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'Which of the following is a characteristic of a command economy?', null, '["A Resources are allocated mainly through the price mechanism.","B Most factors of production are state-owned and resource allocation is decided by central planners.","C The government intervenes only to provide public goods and enforce property rights.","D Consumer sovereignty determines what goods and services are produced."]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-05', 1, '1.2.3', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'When the price of a reusable water bottle rises from £4.00 to £5.00, the quantity demanded per week falls from 200 to 170. Using the percentage change method, what is the price elasticity of demand?', null, '["A -0.6","B -1.67","C -0.15","D -6.0"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-06', 1, '1.2.3', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'When average real incomes rise by 8%, the quantity demanded of a supermarket''s value-range bread falls by 2%. What is the income elasticity of demand (YED) and how should the good be classified?', null, '["A +4.0; a normal luxury good","B +0.25; a normal necessity","C -4.0; an inferior good","D -0.25; an inferior good"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-07', 1, '1.2.3', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'The price of train tickets on a route rises by 10% and, as a result, demand for coach tickets on the same route rises by 5%. What is the cross elasticity of demand (XED) for coach travel with respect to the price of train travel?', null, '["A -0.5, so the goods are complements","B +2.0, so the goods are substitutes","C +0.5, so the goods are substitutes","D -2.0, so the goods are complements"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-08', 1, '1.2.3', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'A bus operator estimates that the price elasticity of demand for its peak-time fares is -0.4. If it raises peak fares by 10%, which outcome is most likely, ceteris paribus?', null, '["A Total revenue falls because fewer passengers travel.","B Quantity demanded falls by 25% and total revenue falls.","C Quantity demanded falls by 4% and total revenue rises.","D Quantity demanded is unchanged and total revenue rises by exactly 10%."]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-09', 1, '1.2.5', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'Which of the following is most likely to make the supply of a manufactured good more price elastic?', null, '["A Producers are operating at full capacity.","B The production process has long lead times.","C Production uses highly specialised factors that are difficult to switch from other uses.","D Producers hold large stocks of the finished good."]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-10', 1, '1.2.6', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'In the market for electric bicycles, demand increases because of a rise in fuel prices, while supply increases because of a fall in the cost of batteries. What is the effect on the equilibrium?', null, '["A Price rises; the effect on quantity is indeterminate.","B Price falls and quantity rises.","C Quantity rises; the effect on price is indeterminate.","D Price and quantity both rise."]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-11', 1, '1.2.8', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'A market has a straight-line demand curve. At a price of £10, quantity demanded is zero. The equilibrium price is £6 and the equilibrium quantity is 400 units. What is the value of consumer surplus?', null, '["A £800","B £1,600","C £2,400","D £4,000"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-12', 1, '1.2.9', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'A specific indirect tax of £3 per unit is imposed on a good. As a result, the price paid by consumers rises from £10 to £12 and the quantity traded falls from 1,000 to 800 units. Which option correctly shows the total tax revenue and the amount of the tax paid by producers?', null, '["A Tax revenue £2,400; paid by producers £800","B Tax revenue £3,000; paid by producers £1,000","C Tax revenue £2,400; paid by producers £1,600","D Tax revenue £3,000; paid by producers £2,000"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-13', 1, '1.2.10', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'When employers were required to enrol workers automatically into a workplace pension (with the option to leave), participation rose sharply even though the financial incentives were unchanged. Which concept best explains this?', null, '["A Computational weakness","B Rational utility maximisation with perfect information","C Altruism","D Default choice as a form of choice architecture"]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-14', 1, '1.3.2', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'In a market where production creates a negative externality and there is no government intervention, which of the following is most likely?', null, '["A Both output and price are above their socially optimal levels.","B Output is above the socially optimal level and price is below it.","C Output is below the socially optimal level and price is above it.","D Output and price are at their socially optimal levels because firms consider all costs."]'::jsonb),
('t1-mcq-15', 1, '1.4.2', 'AS', 'mcq', 1, 'A government sets a maximum rent below the market equilibrium rent. Which outcome would be the clearest example of government failure?', null, '["A Some existing tenants pay lower rents than before.","B Part of producer surplus is transferred to consumers.","C Rented housing becomes more affordable for low-income households who already have tenancies.","D Landlords withdraw properties from the rental market, worsening the shortage of rented housing."]'::jsonb),
