('t1-short-03', 1, '1.2.7', 'AS', 'short', 4, 'Explain how the price mechanism would reallocate resources following a large and sustained increase in demand for electric vehicles.', null, null),
('t1-data-01a', 1, '1.2.5', 'AS', 'data', 5, 'With reference to Table 1, calculate the price elasticity of supply of rental properties in Calderford between 2021 and 2025, and explain what your answer indicates.', 'Calderford rental housing market (illustrative data)

Table 1: Calderford private rental market, 2021-2025 (illustrative data)
Year | Average monthly rent (£) | Rental properties advertised | Median household income after tax (£ per month)
2021 | 900 | 4,000 | 2,500
2022 | 960 | 4,050 | 2,560
2023 | 1,040 | 4,120 | 2,610
2024 | 1,110 | 4,180 | 2,680
2025 | 1,170 | 4,240 | 2,750

Extract A: Pressure on the rental market
Calderford, a fictional university city in the north of England, has seen its student population rise by around a fifth since 2021, while new employers in the life-sciences sector have attracted young professionals to the area. Building new homes is slow: planning applications typically take two years to be approved, and suitable building land near the city centre is scarce. At the same time, higher mortgage interest rates have increased costs for buy-to-let landlords, and in a local survey around 6% of smaller landlords said they plan to sell their properties within two years.

Extract B: A proposed rent cap
Calderford''s council is lobbying for powers to limit rents. Under its proposal, the rent on a new tenancy for a typical two-bedroom property could not exceed £1,000 per month. Supporters argue the cap would protect lower-income tenants, many of whom now spend over 40% of their income on rent. Landlord groups warn that a cap would reduce the number of homes offered for rent and the amount spent maintaining them. Some housing economists argue that the council should instead speed up planning decisions and fund new social housing.', null),
