('t1-data-02a', 1, '1.2.3', 'AS', 'data', 5, 'Using Table 2 and Extract B, calculate the expected percentage change in car journeys into the city centre in the short run and in the long run following the introduction of the charge. Explain what your answers suggest.', 'Westbury congestion charge (illustrative data)

Table 1: Traffic and air quality in Westbury city centre (illustrative data)
Year | Average weekday traffic speed (mph) | Average NO2 concentration (micrograms per cubic metre) | Bus passenger journeys (millions)
2019 | 11.2 | 38 | 42.0
2021 | 10.4 | 41 | 33.5
2023 | 9.6 | 44 | 36.8
2025 | 9.1 | 46 | 38.1

Table 2: Estimated elasticities for Westbury (illustrative data)
Price elasticity of demand for car journeys into the centre: short run -0.3; long run -0.7
Cross elasticity of demand for bus journeys with respect to the cost of a car journey: +0.4
Income elasticity of demand for car journeys: +1.2

Extract A: A city choking on traffic
Westbury, a fictional city of 600,000 people, has some of the slowest rush-hour traffic in its region. Local business groups estimate that delays add around £90 million a year to their costs, and the city''s director of public health links poor air quality to higher rates of childhood asthma. Drivers do not pay for the delays they impose on other road users or for the pollution their vehicles emit.

Extract B: The council''s plan
The council proposes a daily charge of £8 for driving into the city centre between 7am and 7pm on weekdays. For a typical car commuter, this would raise the cost of a return journey by around 40%. Net revenue, estimated at £45 million a year, would be ring-fenced to subsidise bus fares and expand bus lanes. Retailers fear that shoppers will drive to out-of-town retail parks instead, while some residents argue that the charge is unfair to lower-income workers whose shifts start before the buses run.', null),
