('t2-data-01a', 2, '2.1.2', 'AS', 'data', 5, 'With reference to Table 1, explain the difference between disinflation and deflation.', 'UK macroeconomic performance (illustrative data)

Table 1: Selected UK macroeconomic indicators (illustrative data)
Year | Real GDP growth (%) | CPI inflation (%) | Unemployment rate (%) | Current account balance (% of GDP) | Bank Rate at year end (%)
2022 | 3.9 | 8.7 | 3.8 | -3.0 | 3.50
2023 | 0.5 | 6.1 | 4.1 | -3.3 | 5.25
2024 | 0.8 | 2.7 | 4.4 | -2.9 | 4.75
2025 | 1.1 | 3.2 | 4.9 | -2.5 | 4.00

Extract A: From price shock to slow growth
In 2022 wholesale gas prices rose to several times their usual level after supply disruptions, pushing up household energy bills and firms'' production costs. Demand was also recovering strongly after the pandemic, and many firms reported difficulty recruiting staff. The Bank of England responded by raising Bank Rate in a series of steps. By 2024 inflation had fallen close to the 2% target, but the squeeze on real incomes, higher mortgage payments and weak business investment held back growth. Some economists argue that low productivity growth, rather than weak demand, is now the main constraint on the UK economy.

Extract B: Policy choices
The government faces pressure to cut taxes to support households, but it has also committed to a fiscal rule that requires debt to be falling as a share of GDP within five years. Business groups have called for greater investment in transport, digital infrastructure and skills, alongside reform of the planning system, arguing that these would raise the economy''s productive potential.', null),
