('t2-data-02a', 2, '2.4.4', 'AS', 'data', 5, 'Using Extract A, calculate the eventual increase in real GDP resulting from the £25 billion programme, and explain one assumption on which your calculation depends.', 'Investment, infrastructure and growth (illustrative data)

Table 1: Investment (gross fixed capital formation) as a percentage of GDP and annual labour productivity growth, averages 2015-2024 (illustrative data)
Country | Investment (% of GDP) | Labour productivity growth (% per year)
UK | 17.5 | 0.6
Country A | 21.0 | 1.1
Country B | 23.5 | 1.0
Country C | 25.0 | 1.4
Country D | 19.0 | 0.9

Extract A: A five-year infrastructure plan
The government has announced a £25 billion programme of spending on rail upgrades, road schemes, flood defences and broadband in regions with below-average incomes, to be phased in over five years. The Treasury estimates that, in current conditions, the multiplier for this type of capital spending is around 1.6. Construction firms welcome the plan but warn that the industry already has around 40,000 unfilled vacancies and that materials costs remain high.

Extract B: Paying for the plan
The programme will be financed mainly by additional government borrowing. Critics argue this will push up interest rates on government bonds and crowd out private investment. Supporters reply that, with the economy operating below its potential, borrowing to invest in productive assets will pay for itself over time through higher growth and tax revenues. Environmental groups support the flood defences but question the carbon emissions from the new road schemes.', null),
