Bramble and Dunmore, the second- and fourth-largest grocers, have agreed to merge. They say the combined firm would save around £400 million a year through joint purchasing, shared distribution centres and a single IT system, allowing them to compete with the fast-growing discount chains. Grocery retailing typically earns profit margins of around 3% of sales. The two firms have promised to cut prices on 1,000 everyday products if the merger goes ahead.

Extract B: Concerns about market power
Farmers'' organisations warn that the combined firm would buy around 30% of some fresh produce, such as milk and soft fruit, and could use this buying power to push down the prices paid to suppliers. A consumer group points out that in some towns Bramble and Dunmore operate the only two large supermarkets. Northmark''s competition authority has opened an investigation and may block the merger, approve it, or approve it on condition that the firms sell some stores.', null),
('t3-data-02a', 3, '3.5.3', 'A', 'data', 5, 'Using Table 1, calculate the percentage increase in the average hourly pay of care workers and in the national minimum wage between 2021 and 2025. Explain what your answers suggest about the attractiveness of care work.', 'The adult social care labour market (illustrative data)

Table 1: Adult social care workers in England (illustrative data)
Year | Average hourly pay of care workers (£) | National minimum wage for workers aged 21+ (£ per hour) | Vacancy rate (%) | Annual staff turnover (%)
2021 | 9.50 | 9.00 | 6.8 | 29
2022 | 10.10 | 9.60 | 10.6 | 30
2023 | 10.70 | 10.40 | 9.9 | 28
2024 | 11.60 | 11.40 | 8.3 | 25
2025 | 12.40 | 12.20 | 7.0 | 24

Extract A: Pay and conditions
Most care workers are employed by private firms and charities that are paid by local councils to provide care. With council budgets under pressure, the fees paid to providers have often risen more slowly than costs, and many providers say they cannot afford to pay much above the legal minimum. Care work involves unsocial hours, physical and emotional demands, and few opportunities for promotion. In many towns, the local council is the dominant purchaser of care and care providers are among the largest employers of low-paid workers. Some supermarkets and warehouses now pay entry-level staff more than care providers.

Extract B: Rising demand, uncertain supply
Demand for care is rising as the population ages: the number of people aged 85 and over is projected to grow by around 50% over the next 15 years (illustrative projection). Between 2022 and 2024 providers recruited many workers from overseas, which helped reduce vacancies, but tighter visa rules have since reduced this source of recruitment. Options under discussion include a sector-specific minimum wage for care workers set above the national minimum wage, a national pay scale with career progression funded by central government, and more funding for training.', null),
