('t4-data-02a', 4, '4.5.3', 'A', 'data', 5, 'With reference to Table 1, explain why public sector net debt as a share of GDP continued to rise between 2020-21 and 2024-25 even though the budget deficit fell.', 'UK public sector finances (illustrative data)

Table 1: UK public finances (illustrative data)
Financial year | Budget deficit (% of GDP) | Public sector net debt (% of GDP) | Debt interest payments (£ billion) | 10-year government bond yield (%)
2019-20 | 2.6 | 82 | 42 | 0.7
2020-21 | 13.8 | 94 | 27 | 0.5
2022-23 | 4.9 | 96 | 108 | 3.6
2024-25 | 4.6 | 97 | 102 | 4.3

Extract A: From pandemic borrowing to higher interest costs
In 2020-21 the UK''s budget deficit rose to its highest level for decades. Tax revenues fell as output contracted, while spending on job-retention schemes, business support and health rose sharply. Since then, debt interest payments have risen substantially. Around a quarter of government bonds are index-linked, so their interest payments rise with inflation, and higher Bank Rate has increased the cost of new borrowing. Debt interest now costs more each year than the budgets of several major government departments.

Extract B: A fiscal rule under pressure
The government''s fiscal rule requires public sector net debt to be falling as a share of GDP in five years'' time. To meet it, the Chancellor is considering freezing income tax thresholds, cutting public investment and limiting public sector pay rises. Some economists warn that cutting public investment could damage long-run growth, and that the debt ratio depends more on the gap between the interest rate on debt and the growth rate of nominal GDP than on small changes in borrowing. Others argue that high debt leaves the UK vulnerable to sudden rises in bond yields if investors lose confidence.', null),
