insert into public.question_keys (question_id, answer, explanation, mark_scheme) values
('t1-mcq-01', 'B', 'A positive statement is objective and can be tested against evidence. B makes a factual claim that could be checked against attendance data (even if it proved false). A, C and D contain value judgements (''should'', ''unacceptably'', ''ought''), so they are normative.', null),
('t1-mcq-02', 'A', 'Moving from R to S gains 10 units of capital goods but sacrifices 20 units of consumer goods, so each extra unit costs 20 / 10 = 2 consumer goods. The opportunity cost rises (1, then 1.5, then 2) because resources are not equally suited to both goods, giving a PPF that is concave to the origin.', null),
('t1-mcq-03', 'C', 'Expressing the values of different goods in a common unit so they can be compared is the unit of account function. Money acts as a medium of exchange only when the purchase actually takes place.', null),
('t1-mcq-04', 'B', 'In a command (planned) economy the state owns most resources and planners decide what, how and for whom to produce. A, C and D describe features of a free market economy.', null),
('t1-mcq-05', 'A', '%ΔQd = -30 / 200 = -15%; %ΔP = 1 / 4 = +25%. PED = -15 / 25 = -0.6, so demand is price inelastic. An answer of -1.67 inverts the formula.', null),
('t1-mcq-06', 'D', 'YED = %ΔQd / %ΔY = -2 / 8 = -0.25. A negative YED means demand falls as income rises, so the good is inferior.', null),
('t1-mcq-07', 'C', 'XED = %ΔQd of coach travel / %ΔP of train travel = +5 / +10 = +0.5. A positive XED indicates substitutes; the value below 1 suggests they are fairly weak substitutes.', null),
('t1-mcq-08', 'C', '%ΔQd = -0.4 x 10% = -4%. Because demand is price inelastic, the percentage rise in price exceeds the percentage fall in quantity, so total revenue rises (by roughly 1.10 x 0.96 = 1.056, about 5.6%).', null),
('t1-mcq-09', 'D', 'Large stocks allow firms to increase quantity supplied quickly when price rises, making supply more elastic. Full capacity, long lead times and immobile factors all make supply less responsive.', null),
('t1-mcq-10', 'C', 'Both a rightward shift of demand and a rightward shift of supply raise equilibrium quantity. Demand pushes price up while supply pushes it down, so the net effect on price depends on the relative size of the two shifts.', null),
('t1-mcq-11', 'A', 'Consumer surplus is the triangle between the demand curve and the price: 0.5 x (£10 - £6) x 400 = £800. An answer of £1,600 forgets to halve the rectangle.', null),
('t1-mcq-12', 'A', 'Revenue = £3 x 800 = £2,400. Consumers pay £2 more per unit (£1,600 in total), so producers bear the remaining £1 per unit, receiving £9 net: £1 x 800 = £800.', null),
('t1-mcq-13', 'D', 'Behavioural economics suggests people tend to stick with the default option because of inertia and bounded rationality. Changing the default from ''opt in'' to ''opt out'' is a nudge through choice architecture; a purely rational agent would not change behaviour when incentives are unchanged.', null),
('t1-mcq-14', 'B', 'Firms base supply on marginal private cost, which is below marginal social cost. The free-market supply curve therefore lies to the right of the social cost curve, so output is too high and price too low compared with the social optimum where MSC = MSB.', null),
('t1-mcq-15', 'D', 'Government failure occurs when intervention leads to a net welfare loss or a worse allocation of resources. A fall in quantity supplied and a growing shortage is an unintended consequence that undermines the policy''s aim. The other options are intended distributional effects.', null),
('t1-short-01', null, null, 'Knowledge: Definition of a specific (per-unit) indirect tax and of tax incidence (how the burden is shared between consumers and producers). Understanding that the tax shifts the supply curve vertically upwards by the amount of the tax.
Application: Accurate diagram: steep (inelastic) demand curve; supply shifts from S to S + tax; new equilibrium shows a large rise in the consumer price and only a small fall in the price received by producers; consumer and producer incidence areas correctly labelled. A relevant example such as tobacco, petrol or alcohol.
Analysis: Because consumers have few close substitutes, quantity demanded falls only slightly when price rises, so producers can pass most of the tax on through higher prices without losing many sales. The vertical distance between the new price and the original price (consumer share) is therefore greater than the fall in the net price received by producers.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge (definition of incidence or specific tax); up to 2 marks for application (accurately labelled diagram showing incidence areas, or a relevant example); up to 2 marks for analysis (linked explanation of why low PED allows producers to pass the tax on).'),
('t1-short-02', null, null, 'Knowledge: Asymmetric information: one party to a transaction has more or better information than the other. Market failure: the free market leads to a misallocation of resources.
Application: Sellers of used cars know whether a car is reliable or faulty, but buyers cannot easily tell before purchase.
Analysis: Buyers are only willing to pay a price reflecting average quality. Owners of good-quality cars are unwilling to sell at this price and withdraw from the market, so the average quality of cars offered falls, which further lowers what buyers will pay (adverse selection). Fewer good-quality cars are traded than is socially optimal, so there is under-consumption and a welfare loss, or buyers pay too much for poor cars.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge (definition of asymmetric information or market failure); 1 mark for application to used cars; up to 2 marks for analysis (a developed chain explaining how the information gap leads to a misallocation of resources).'),
('t1-short-03', null, null, 'Knowledge: Functions of the price mechanism: signalling, incentive and rationing.
Application: Applied to electric vehicles: rising demand from consumers switching away from petrol and diesel cars; inputs such as batteries and charging equipment.
Analysis: At the original price there is excess demand, so price rises. The higher price signals to producers that consumers value EVs more; higher profits provide an incentive to expand output and for new firms to enter, so resources (labour, capital, raw materials) move from petrol car production into EV production. Meanwhile the higher price rations the limited supply to consumers who are most willing and able to pay, until a new equilibrium is reached.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge of a function of the price mechanism; 1 mark for application to the EV market; up to 2 marks for analysis showing how the price change leads to reallocation of resources (at least two linked functions).'),
('t1-data-01a', null, null, 'Knowledge: PES = % change in quantity supplied / % change in price. Understanding of elastic versus inelastic supply.
Application: % change in quantity = (4,240 - 4,000) / 4,000 x 100 = +6%. % change in price = (1,170 - 900) / 900 x 100 = +30%. PES = 6 / 30 = 0.2 (price inelastic).
Analysis: A 30% rise in rents brought only a 6% increase in properties offered, so supply is highly unresponsive. This reflects the two-year planning delays and scarce building land (Extract A): the housing stock cannot be expanded quickly. As a result, increases in demand lead mainly to higher rents rather than more homes. Credit may be given for noting that the figure assumes other supply factors were constant, whereas higher mortgage costs may also have shifted supply.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for the PES formula; 2 marks for application (1 for correct percentage changes, 1 for PES = 0.2); up to 2 marks for analysis explaining what an inelastic PES means in this market.'),
('t1-data-01b', null, null, 'Knowledge: Determinants of demand (population, income, tastes) and of supply (costs of production, number of suppliers, expectations). Equilibrium price determination.
Application: Student population up by about a fifth; arrival of life-sciences employers; median income up 10% (£2,500 to £2,750); higher mortgage rates raising landlords'' costs; 6% of small landlords intending to sell; average rent up 30%.
Analysis: Reason 1: rising demand. More students and young professionals shift demand for rented housing to the right; with inelastic supply (PES about 0.2), most of the adjustment is a rise in price rather than quantity. Diagram: D shifts right against a steep supply curve, price rises from P1 to P2. Reason 2: falling or slower-growing supply. Higher mortgage interest raises the cost of providing rented property, and some landlords plan to leave the market, shifting supply left (or limiting its growth), which further raises equilibrium rent.
Evaluation: Relative importance: incomes rose by only 10% while rents rose 30%, suggesting population-driven demand and supply constraints matter more than income. The data show advertised properties, which may not capture the full stock. Landlord exits are only intentions and may not happen; properties sold may become owner-occupied, reducing renter demand too. General inflation over the period means part of the rise is nominal rather than a real increase. Short-run versus long-run: supply may become more elastic over time.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 5 marks; Evaluation up to 3 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-2 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (3-5 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (6-8 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t1-data-01c', null, null, 'Knowledge: A maximum price is a legal upper limit on price; it is effective only if set below the equilibrium price.
Application: Current average rent is £1,170, so a £1,000 cap is about £170 (roughly 15%) below equilibrium and would be binding. Tenants spending over 40% of income on rent; landlord warnings about supply and maintenance.
Analysis: Diagram with maximum price below equilibrium: quantity demanded rises (Qd) and quantity supplied falls (Qs), creating excess demand (a shortage). Tenants who secure a property gain consumer surplus at landlords'' expense; there is a deadweight welfare loss from fewer transactions. Non-price rationing follows: waiting lists, landlords selecting ''safer'' tenants, or illegal side-payments. Landlords may cut maintenance, so quality falls. Some may convert to short-term holiday lets or sell.
Evaluation: The size of the shortage depends on elasticities: with inelastic supply in the short run (PES about 0.2), the fall in quantity supplied may be small at first, but supply could become more elastic and fall further in the long run. The cap applies only to new tenancies and to a ''typical'' two-bedroom property, so landlords may keep existing tenants longer or reclassify properties. Effectiveness depends on enforcement. Equity gains for low-income tenants may be judged to outweigh efficiency losses.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 6 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-3 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (4-6 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (7-10 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t1-data-01d', null, null, 'Knowledge: Government failure: intervention that results in a net welfare loss or a less efficient allocation of resources than before. Causes include distortion of price signals, unintended consequences, excessive administrative costs and information gaps.
Application: 6% of small landlords already intend to sell; scarce land and slow planning; rents £170 above the proposed cap; council may lack information on the equilibrium rent for different property types.
Analysis: Distortion of price signals: the cap reduces the return on rental housing, discouraging investment in new rental supply and maintenance, so the long-run shortage could worsen. Unintended consequences: landlords exit or switch to unregulated uses; black-market payments; discrimination in tenant selection. Information gaps: the council may set the cap at the wrong level for different areas and property sizes. Administrative and enforcement costs reduce net benefit.
Evaluation: The degree of failure depends on how far below equilibrium the cap is set; a cap close to market rent may have limited effects. With very inelastic short-run supply, landlords may not be able to switch easily, limiting reduction in supply. Properties sold by landlords may be bought by former tenants, so the housing stock is not lost. Design features such as exempting new-build properties or indexing the cap to inflation can reduce damage. Even if allocative efficiency falls, a judgement on equity may support the policy. Overall, failure is likely in the long run unless supply-side measures accompany the cap.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 8 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-4 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (5-8 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (9-12 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t1-data-01e', null, null, 'Knowledge: Relevant methods of intervention: state provision (social housing), subsidies (to builders or tenants), regulation (planning reform), indirect taxes (e.g. on empty or second homes), provision of information.
Application: Two-year planning delays, scarce land near the centre, rising student and worker population, tenants spending over 40% of income on rent, 6% of landlords planning to sell.
Analysis: Planning reform: faster approvals make supply more price elastic and shift supply right over time, lowering rents. Direct provision of social housing: increases supply at below-market rents for low-income households. Subsidies to house builders: lower costs, shift supply right. Housing benefit or rent subsidies to tenants: raise effective purchasing power, but shift demand right. Taxes on empty homes or short-term lets: bring existing properties back into the long-term rental market. Purpose-built student accommodation: reduces student demand for general rented housing.
Evaluation: Time lags: new supply takes years, while affordability problems are immediate. Opportunity cost and funding: social housing and subsidies require public money that could be spent elsewhere. Incidence: with inelastic supply, much of a tenant subsidy may be captured by landlords through higher rents. Land is physically scarce near the centre, limiting supply responses; transport links to cheaper areas may be an alternative. A combination of short-term support for vulnerable tenants and long-term supply measures is likely to be most effective. Justified judgement on which policy is most appropriate.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 9 marks; Evaluation up to 6 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-5 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (6-10 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (11-15 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t1-data-02a', null, null, 'Knowledge: PED = % change in quantity demanded / % change in price, so % change in quantity = PED x % change in price. Short-run demand tends to be less elastic than long-run demand.
Application: Short run: -0.3 x 40% = -12%. Long run: -0.7 x 40% = -28%.
Analysis: Demand for car journeys is price inelastic, especially in the short run, because many drivers have no convenient alternative; traffic falls only modestly at first, while the charge raises substantial revenue. Over time drivers adjust (changing jobs, moving home, car sharing, switching to buses), so demand becomes more elastic and the reduction in traffic is larger.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge (rearranged PED formula); 2 marks for application (1 mark each for -12% and -28%); up to 2 marks for analysis of why the response differs between short and long run or what this implies for the charge.'),
('t1-data-02b', null, null, 'Knowledge: A negative externality is a cost imposed on a third party not involved in the transaction, for which no compensation is paid. Social cost = private cost + external cost. Social optimum where MSC = MSB.
Application: External costs: congestion delays costing businesses around £90 million a year; NO2 rising from 38 to 46 micrograms per cubic metre; links to childhood asthma; drivers do not pay for these effects.
Analysis: Diagram: MPC below MSC with the gap representing marginal external cost; drivers choose Q1 where MPC = MPB (demand), but the socially optimal number of journeys is Q* where MSC = MSB. Overconsumption (Q1 > Q*) creates a deadweight welfare loss triangle. Drivers consider only their own fuel and time costs, so roads are over-used, slowing speeds (11.2 to 9.1 mph) and worsening air quality.
Evaluation: Part of congestion is an internal cost borne by drivers themselves, so the external element may be smaller than the £90 million suggests; the figure comes from business groups who may overstate it. The external cost varies by time of day and location, so a single diagram simplifies. Measuring health damage in money terms is difficult. Other sources (buses, deliveries, heating) also contribute to NO2.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 5 marks; Evaluation up to 3 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-2 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (3-5 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (6-8 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t1-data-02c', null, null, 'Knowledge: Meaning of PED, XED and YED; significance of sign and size.
Application: PED -0.3 (short run) and -0.7 (long run); XED for buses +0.4; YED for car journeys +1.2; 40% increase in journey cost; £45 million revenue.
Analysis: Inelastic PED means traffic falls only around 12% in the short run, limiting the immediate impact on congestion and pollution but generating significant revenue to fund alternatives. The larger long-run response (around 28%) suggests success grows over time. XED of +0.4 means bus demand rises by about 16% (0.4 x 40%), so bus capacity must expand or buses become overcrowded. YED of +1.2 means car journeys are income elastic: as incomes grow, demand shifts right, eroding the effect of a fixed £8 charge, so the charge may need to rise over time.
Evaluation: Estimates are uncertain and may not hold for a 40% price change. Elasticities differ between groups: shift workers without bus alternatives may have very inelastic demand, so the charge acts mainly as a tax on them. ''Success'' depends on the objective: reducing traffic versus raising revenue (a more elastic response reduces revenue). Non-price factors such as bus reliability may matter more than price.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 6 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-3 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (4-6 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (7-10 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t1-data-02d', null, null, 'Knowledge: A subsidy is a payment by the government to producers that lowers costs and shifts supply to the right. Public transport may generate positive externalities by reducing car use.
Application: £45 million annual revenue; bus journeys fell from 42.0 million (2019) to 38.1 million (2025); XED +0.4; concern about lower-income workers.
Analysis: Subsidy diagram: supply of bus services shifts right, fares fall and passenger numbers rise. Lower fares make buses a closer substitute, increasing the shift away from cars and strengthening the effect of the charge. Hypothecation increases public acceptance of the charge. Equity: lower-income households, who use buses more, benefit, reducing the regressive impact of the charge.
Evaluation: Opportunity cost: the £45 million could fund other priorities. If demand for bus travel is price inelastic, much of the subsidy benefits existing passengers rather than attracting drivers. Frequency, reliability and coverage (e.g. early shifts) may matter more than fares, so spending on bus lanes or routes might be more effective. Revenue will fall as the charge succeeds in reducing traffic. Risk of inefficiency if operators receive subsidies without performance conditions. Judgement on whether the subsidy should be targeted.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 8 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-4 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (5-8 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (9-12 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t1-data-02e', null, null, 'Knowledge: A charge acts like an indirect tax to internalise an external cost. Regulation sets legal limits or bans backed by penalties. Both aim to move output towards the social optimum.
Application: Congestion costs of £90 million; NO2 rising; £8 charge raising journey cost by 40%; retailers'' and low-income workers'' concerns.
Analysis: Congestion charge: raises the private cost of driving towards the social cost (diagram showing MPC shifting up towards MSC), reduces all journeys, generates revenue to fund alternatives, and leaves drivers free to choose. Regulation: guarantees removal of the most polluting vehicles, so targets NO2 directly; simple to understand; no need to estimate the money value of the externality. However, it does nothing to reduce congestion from newer cars and may encourage drivers to buy new vehicles rather than drive less.
Evaluation: Effectiveness depends on the objective: the charge is better targeted at congestion, while regulation may be better for air quality. Setting the right charge requires accurate information about external costs; regulation requires knowing the right standard. Enforcement costs (cameras) apply to both. Equity: bans fall heavily on low-income owners of older cars, while the charge falls on anyone who must drive. Possible displacement of shoppers to out-of-town parks. A combined approach (charge plus emission-based rates) may be best. Justified overall judgement.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 9 marks; Evaluation up to 6 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-5 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
