Level 4 (19-25 marks): Precise, wide-ranging knowledge applied throughout to well-chosen examples; fully developed, logical chains of reasoning with accurate, integrated diagrams; sustained evaluation that weighs arguments, prioritises the most significant factors and reaches a fully justified overall judgement.'),
('t1-essay-03', null, null, 'Knowledge: Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable; the free-rider problem means private firms cannot charge, so the market fails to provide them (a missing market). Quasi-public goods have some but not all of these characteristics. State provision is funded through taxation.
Application: Street lighting and flood defences as near-pure public goods; roads as quasi-public goods that become rival when congested and can be made excludable through tolls or road pricing; national defence.
Analysis: Without the state, flood defences and street lighting would be under-provided or not provided at all because no one can be excluded and individuals have an incentive to free ride. State provision funded by compulsory taxation overcomes this and can bring provision closer to the socially optimal level, with large benefits to households and firms. Some public goods also generate external benefits (e.g. lower crime from lighting).
Evaluation: Government failure: the state lacks information on how much people value public goods, so it may over- or under-provide; political pressure may direct spending to marginal constituencies; lack of profit motive may reduce efficiency. Opportunity cost of taxation. The state can fund without producing: private firms can build and operate under contract, or through public-private partnerships. Technology can make goods excludable (electronic tolls), allowing market provision for quasi-public goods such as roads. Community and voluntary provision is possible for small-scale goods. Judgement: strong case for state funding of pure public goods, weaker for quasi-public goods.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
Levels:
