Level 3 (11-15 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t2-data-02a', null, null, 'Knowledge: The multiplier: the final change in national income = multiplier x initial change in injections.
Application: 1.6 x £25 billion = £40 billion increase in real GDP (over the life of the programme).
Analysis: Assumptions include: there is spare capacity, so extra demand raises real output rather than prices (the 40,000 vacancies suggest this may not hold in construction); there is no crowding out of private investment by higher interest rates; the marginal propensities to withdraw stay constant; other components of AD are unchanged (ceteris paribus).
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge of the multiplier process; 2 marks for application (correct calculation of £40 billion, with 1 mark for method); up to 2 marks for analysis of one assumption and why it matters.'),
('t2-data-02b', null, null, 'Knowledge: Labour productivity: output per worker or per hour. Investment increases the capital stock. Causes of long-run growth.
Application: UK investment of 17.5% of GDP, the lowest in Table 1, with the lowest productivity growth (0.6% a year); Country C has the highest investment (25.0%) and productivity growth (1.4%).
Analysis: Reason 1: capital deepening. More capital per worker (machinery, IT, transport links) allows each worker to produce more output. Reason 2: new technology is often embodied in new capital, so low investment slows the adoption of more efficient techniques. Both shift LRAS right; low investment therefore limits the growth of productive potential.
Evaluation: Correlation is not causation: high productivity growth may lead to investment (accelerator effect). The relationship is not exact: Country B invests more than Country A but has slower productivity growth. Other factors matter, including skills, management quality, the sector mix (services versus manufacturing) and the efficiency of investment. Averages over ten years may hide changes.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 5 marks; Evaluation up to 3 marks.
