('t2-data-02c', null, null, 'Knowledge: Government spending is a component of AD; infrastructure increases productive capacity and shifts LRAS. Distinction between short-run and long-run effects.
Application: £25 billion programme over five years; multiplier of 1.6; economy described as below its potential; 40,000 vacancies in construction; high materials costs.
Analysis: Short run: higher G shifts AD right; with spare capacity, real output rises with a small rise in the price level; multiplier effects increase the impact. Long run: improved transport, broadband and flood protection raise productivity and reduce costs, shifting LRAS right, raising potential output and easing inflationary pressure (diagram showing both shifts: higher real output, price level potentially back towards original).
Evaluation: Bottlenecks in construction (labour shortages, high materials costs) mean more of the short-run effect may be on prices than output, at least in that sector. The size of the programme (£5 billion a year) is small relative to UK GDP, limiting the macro effect. Long-run benefits depend on project choice and delivery; delays and cost overruns are common. Crowding out could offset part of the AD increase.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 6 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-3 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
