Level 3 (7-10 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t2-data-02d', null, null, 'Knowledge: Impacts of economic growth on consumers, firms, government, living standards, the environment and the distribution of income.
Application: Spending targeted at regions with below-average incomes; flood defences supported by environmental groups; concerns about carbon emissions from road schemes; construction vacancies.
Analysis: Living standards: construction jobs and multiplier effects raise incomes locally; better transport and broadband attract firms and widen access to jobs; flood defences protect homes and businesses. Environment: flood defences reduce damage from extreme weather; rail and broadband may reduce car use and commuting; road schemes increase traffic and emissions, plus emissions from construction itself.
Evaluation: Benefits depend on whether jobs go to local residents or to workers from elsewhere, given skills shortages. Better transport links can also draw activity away from poorer regions towards cities. Environmental impact depends on the mix of projects. Long time lags before benefits appear. Real GDP is an imperfect measure of living standards; wellbeing gains from flood protection may be large but not fully captured. Overall judgement on net effect.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 8 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
