('t2-data-02e', null, null, 'Knowledge: Fiscal policy; budget deficit and national debt; crowding out; the multiplier.
Application: £25 billion programme; economy below potential; critics'' concerns about bond yields and crowding out; supporters'' argument that productive investment pays for itself.
Analysis: Borrowing: injects extra demand without reducing households'' disposable income, so the net boost to AD is larger than if financed by taxes (which would reduce consumption and partly offset the multiplier). With spare capacity, crowding out is less likely. Investment in productive assets raises future output and tax revenue, which can service the debt. Future generations benefit from infrastructure, so it is fair that they share the cost. Taxation: avoids adding to debt and interest costs; but higher taxes may reduce incentives to work and invest.
Evaluation: Crowding out depends on the output gap and on whether higher borrowing raises interest rates; if bond markets lose confidence, yields could rise sharply. Debt interest has an opportunity cost. The return on infrastructure is uncertain and may not cover borrowing costs. Fiscal rules may limit borrowing. The type of tax matters: taxes on wealth or carbon may have smaller disincentive effects. Judgement depends on the state of the economy and the quality of projects.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 9 marks; Evaluation up to 6 marks.
