('t2-essay-01', null, null, 'Knowledge: Quantitative easing (QE): the central bank creates new money electronically to buy assets, mainly government bonds, from financial institutions. Monetary policy transmission. Economic growth as an increase in real GDP.
Application: Use of QE by the Bank of England after the 2008 financial crisis and during the pandemic when Bank Rate was close to zero; later reversal through quantitative tightening.
Analysis: QE raises bond prices and lowers long-term interest rates, reducing borrowing costs for firms and households, encouraging investment and consumption. Higher asset prices create a wealth effect. Banks'' reserves increase, potentially raising lending. Lower yields may weaken sterling, boosting net exports. AD shifts right, increasing real output when there is a negative output gap (diagram).
Evaluation: Effectiveness depends on confidence: in a recession, banks may hoard reserves and firms may not borrow (''pushing on a string''). Much of the effect may raise asset prices rather than real output, increasing wealth inequality. Risk of inflation if used when the economy is near capacity. Effects are uncertain and hard to measure; the counterfactual is unknown. QE affects AD, not LRAS, so it does not address long-run growth constraints such as productivity. Exit from QE may create losses and volatility. Overall judgement, e.g. effective at preventing deeper recessions but limited for sustained growth.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, often in the form of definitions; little application to examples or the economy; analysis is descriptive or confused; evaluation, if attempted, is unsupported assertion.
Level 2 (7-12 marks): Accurate knowledge with some relevant application; chains of reasoning are developed for one or two points but are partial or one-sided; diagrams may be present but are not fully explained; evaluation is limited and generic.
Level 3 (13-18 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to relevant, realistic examples; several developed chains of reasoning with appropriate diagrams; evaluation is present across the answer, considering alternative outcomes, but is not consistently supported or prioritised.
Level 4 (19-25 marks): Precise, wide-ranging knowledge applied throughout to well-chosen examples; fully developed, logical chains of reasoning with accurate, integrated diagrams; sustained evaluation that weighs arguments, prioritises the most significant factors and reaches a fully justified overall judgement.'),
('t2-essay-02', null, null, 'Knowledge: Types of unemployment: structural, frictional, cyclical (demand-deficient), seasonal. Supply-side policies (market-based and interventionist) and demand-side policies.
Application: Regional and skills mismatches, e.g. in former industrial areas; apprenticeships and retraining; Universal Credit and work incentives; job centres and information; labour shortages in some sectors alongside unemployment in others.
Analysis: Supply-side policies such as education and training reduce structural unemployment by improving occupational mobility; better job-matching information reduces frictional unemployment; reforms to benefits and lower income tax raise incentives to work; housing and transport policies improve geographical mobility. They shift LRAS right, allowing higher employment without inflation.
Evaluation: Supply-side policies are ineffective against cyclical unemployment, where demand-side fiscal and monetary policy are more appropriate (e.g. during recessions). Long time lags and high costs. Market-based policies such as cutting benefits may increase poverty and inequality. Effectiveness depends on the type of unemployment that dominates at the time; data on vacancies versus unemployment can help diagnose this. The most effective approach is likely to combine policies. Justified judgement.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
