('t2-essay-03', null, null, 'Knowledge: Real GDP per capita: inflation-adjusted output per person. Purchasing power parities (PPP). Alternative measures such as the Human Development Index and measures of national wellbeing.
Application: Comparisons between high-income and middle-income countries; differences in hours worked, income distribution and environmental quality; the ONS measures of national wellbeing.
Analysis: Real GDP per capita measures the average quantity of goods and services available per person, which is closely linked to material living standards; it is widely available and calculated using a common methodology, making comparisons feasible. Converting at PPP rather than market exchange rates adjusts for differences in the cost of living.
Evaluation: Limitations: it is an average and ignores inequality; it omits non-market output (household work, volunteering) and the hidden economy, which vary between countries; it does not reflect leisure time, working conditions, health, education or environmental damage; the composition of output (e.g. defence spending) matters; data quality varies. Alternatives such as HDI include health and education, while wellbeing surveys measure subjective life satisfaction, but these have their own weaknesses. Judgement: useful starting point but best used alongside other indicators.
