('t3-mcq-01', 'D', 'Total revenue rises from £2,000 (100 x £20) to £2,160 (120 x £18), an increase of £160. Marginal revenue per extra unit = £160 / 20 = £8. MR is below price because the price cut applies to all units sold.', null),
('t3-mcq-02', 'C', 'Total cost = £500 + £1,000 = £1,500. ATC = £1,500 / 50 = £30 (made up of AFC £10 and AVC £20).', null),
('t3-mcq-03', 'A', 'External economies of scale arise from the growth of the industry or area as a whole, lowering costs for all firms in it. The other options are internal economies (purchasing, technical/R&D and financial) arising from the growth of the individual firm.', null),
('t3-mcq-04', 'B', 'The firm makes a loss of £2 per unit, but each unit sold covers its variable cost and contributes £2 towards fixed costs, which must be paid even if it shuts down. The short-run shutdown point is where price falls below AVC.', null),
('t3-mcq-05', 'C', 'The car maker is integrating with a firm at an earlier stage of its own production process (a supplier of inputs), which is backward vertical integration.', null),
('t3-mcq-06', 'A', 'Total revenue is maximised when an extra unit adds nothing to revenue, i.e. MR = 0. MC = MR is the profit-maximising condition, and AC = AR is the sales volume maximisation condition (subject to making at least normal profit).', null),
('t3-mcq-07', 'A', 'At P = MC, the value consumers place on the last unit equals the cost of the resources used to produce it, so resources are allocated to best meet consumer wants. Minimum AC is the condition for productive efficiency.', null),
('t3-mcq-08', 'D', 'Free entry and exit compete away supernormal profits and losses, so in the long run each firm earns normal profit at the lowest point of its AC curve, achieving both productive and allocative efficiency.', null),
('t3-mcq-09', 'C', 'The four-firm concentration ratio is the combined market share of the four largest firms: 28 + 20 + 14 + 12 = 74%, indicating an oligopolistic market.', null),
('t3-mcq-10', 'B', 'Whatever Y does, X earns more by pricing low (14 > 10 and 6 > 4), and the same applies to Y. Both choose low price, giving a Nash equilibrium of (6, 6), even though both would be better off colluding on a high price: a prisoners'' dilemma.', null),
('t3-mcq-11', 'D', 'Price discrimination requires price-setting power, different PEDs in separate sub-markets, and the ability to prevent arbitrage (resale). Producing at minimum average cost is not required.', null),
('t3-mcq-12', 'B', 'A monopsonist''s marginal cost of labour lies above the supply curve because it must raise the wage for all workers to hire one more. It employs where MRP = MCL, which is fewer workers than in a competitive market, and pays the wage given by the supply curve at that level, which is lower.', null),
('t3-mcq-13', 'A', 'A market is contestable when the threat of entry is strong. Low sunk costs make ''hit-and-run'' entry possible, forcing incumbents to keep prices close to average cost even if there are few firms.', null),
('t3-mcq-14', 'C', 'MRP = marginal physical product x marginal revenue = 10 units x £15 = £150. In perfect competition MR equals the market price.', null),
('t3-mcq-15', 'B', 'The CMA investigates mergers and markets and enforces competition law. Bank Rate is set by the Bank of England, the National Living Wage by the government, and water price limits by the industry regulator, Ofwat.', null),
('t3-short-01', null, null, 'Knowledge: Internal economies of scale: falls in long-run average cost resulting from the growth of the firm itself. Types include purchasing, technical, managerial, financial, marketing and risk-bearing economies.
Application: Applied to a supermarket chain: bulk buying of food and household goods; large automated distribution centres; national advertising campaigns; access to cheaper finance.
Analysis: Purchasing: buying very large volumes gives bargaining power to negotiate lower prices per unit from suppliers, lowering average costs. Technical: large distribution centres and automated stock systems have high fixed costs that can be spread over a very large volume of sales, reducing cost per item. Marketing: the cost of a national TV campaign is spread across many stores, so advertising cost per unit sold falls.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: Up to 2 marks for each economy of scale: 1 mark for identifying a relevant internal economy and 1 mark for explaining how it reduces average cost for a supermarket.'),
('t3-short-02', null, null, 'Knowledge: Monopsony: a single (or dominant) buyer of a factor such as labour. The marginal cost of labour (MCL) lies above the average cost of labour (the supply curve) because hiring an extra worker requires a higher wage for all workers.
Application: Diagram with supply of labour (ACL), MCL above it, and MRP (demand for labour). Competitive outcome at W1, Q1 where MRP = supply; monopsony employs Q2 where MRP = MCL and pays W2 from the supply curve, with W2 < W1 and Q2 < Q1. Example such as a dominant local employer or a public-sector employer.
Analysis: The profit-maximising monopsonist hires workers up to the point where the extra cost of the last worker (MCL) equals the extra revenue they generate (MRP). Because MCL rises faster than the wage, this occurs at a lower level of employment. It then only needs to pay the wage at which Q2 workers are willing to work, read from the supply curve, so wages are below MRP and below the competitive level.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge (definition of monopsony or MCL); up to 2 marks for application (accurately labelled diagram); up to 2 marks for analysis explaining why employment and wages are lower.'),
('t3-short-03', null, null, 'Knowledge: Supernormal profit is profit above normal profit, i.e. where AR > AC. Monopolistic competition has many firms, differentiated products and low barriers to entry.
Application: Supernormal profit = (AR - ATC) x Q = (£12 - £9) x 2,000 = £6,000.
Analysis: Supernormal profit attracts new firms because barriers to entry are low. As new firms offer similar but differentiated products, each existing firm''s share of market demand falls and its demand (AR) curve shifts left and becomes more elastic. This continues until AR is tangential to AC, where only normal profit is earned.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge (definition of supernormal profit or the low-barrier feature of the market); 2 marks for application (method and correct answer of £6,000); up to 2 marks for analysis of how entry erodes supernormal profit.'),
('t3-data-01a', null, null, 'Knowledge: The n-firm concentration ratio is the combined market share of the n largest firms. A high concentration ratio indicates an oligopolistic market.
Application: Before: Alpha 26 + Bramble 18 + Cornerway 15 + Dunmore 12 = 71%. After: Bramble-Dunmore 30 + Alpha 26 + Cornerway 15 + Easysave 10 = 81%.
Analysis: The market is already highly concentrated (oligopoly) and the merger would increase concentration further, creating a new market leader with 30%. Fewer, larger firms may find it easier to recognise their interdependence and avoid price competition. However, the concentration ratio says nothing about the strength of competition from the growing discounters or about local market concentration.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge of the concentration ratio; 2 marks for application (1 mark for 71%, 1 mark for 81%); up to 2 marks for analysis of what the figures indicate.'),
('t3-data-01b', null, null, 'Knowledge: Reasons for business growth: economies of scale, increased market power, survival/defence against competitors, diversification, managerial objectives. Horizontal integration.
Application: Cost savings of £400 million a year; discounters'' combined share rising from 12% to 18%; both firms losing market share; thin 3% profit margins.
Analysis: Economies of scale: purchasing economies from joint buying and technical economies from shared distribution and IT reduce LRAC, increasing profit at given prices or allowing price cuts. Market power and survival: combining shares (30%) makes the firm the market leader, giving greater pricing power and buying power over suppliers, and a stronger position against discounters who have been taking market share.
Evaluation: Claimed cost savings are often overstated; integration costs and diseconomies of scale (communication, culture clashes) may reduce them. With margins of only 3%, £400 million is significant relative to profits. Managers may pursue growth for its own sake (principal-agent problem). The relative importance of cost savings versus market power is key for the competition authority.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 5 marks; Evaluation up to 3 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-2 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (3-5 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (6-8 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t3-data-01c', null, null, 'Knowledge: Monopsony power: a dominant buyer can pay suppliers a price below the competitive level. MC of supply above average cost (supply) curve.
Application: Combined firm buying around 30% of some fresh produce such as milk and soft fruit; farmers'' organisations'' warnings.
Analysis: Monopsony diagram: the buyer purchases where its marginal value equals the marginal cost of supply, buying a smaller quantity at a lower price than in a competitive market. Farmers'' revenues and producer surplus fall; less efficient farms may exit; reduced incentive to invest in quality. Lower input costs may raise the supermarket''s profit or be passed on to consumers.
Evaluation: 30% is significant but farmers still have other buyers (other supermarkets, processors, export markets), limiting monopsony power. Farmers can create countervailing power through co-operatives. Perishable products like milk and soft fruit give farmers weak bargaining positions. If lower costs are passed on as lower prices (1,000 products), consumers benefit. Regulation such as a supplier code of practice could limit abuse.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 6 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-3 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (4-6 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (7-10 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t3-data-01d', null, null, 'Knowledge: Competition policy: merger control, the aim of preventing a substantial lessening of competition; static and dynamic efficiency; consumer and producer surplus; possible remedies.
Application: CR4 rising from 71% to 81%; £400 million cost savings; promised price cuts; towns where the two firms are the only large supermarkets; 30% of some produce purchases; discounters gaining share.
Analysis: Case for blocking: greater market power could lead to higher prices, reduced choice and lower quality, especially in local markets with no alternative; allocative inefficiency (P > MC); monopsony power over farmers; reduced incentive to innovate. Case for allowing: economies of scale reduce costs and may lower prices; the merged firm could compete more effectively with discounters; the market may remain contestable given discounter growth; dynamic efficiency if savings fund investment.
Evaluation: The outcome depends on whether cost savings are passed on to consumers; promises are hard to enforce. Local versus national markets: concentration matters most where the firms overlap. A conditional approval requiring store sales in overlapping towns may address the main concern while preserving efficiency gains. Risks of regulatory error and information gaps. Discounters'' rapid growth suggests barriers to entry are not insurmountable. Justified judgement, e.g. approve with conditions.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 9 marks; Evaluation up to 6 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-5 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (6-10 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (11-15 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t3-data-02a', null, null, 'Knowledge: Percentage change = (new - old) / old x 100. Wage differentials influence the supply of labour to an occupation.
Application: Care workers: (12.40 - 9.50) / 9.50 x 100 = 30.5%. Minimum wage: (12.20 - 9.00) / 9.00 x 100 = 35.6%. The premium over the minimum fell from 50p (5.6%) to 20p (1.6%).
Analysis: Care pay has been driven largely by the legal minimum rather than by market forces, and the differential over minimum-wage jobs elsewhere has narrowed. Given the demanding nature of care work, there is little financial incentive to choose it over less demanding jobs such as retail or warehouse work, which reduces the supply of labour to the sector.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge (percentage change method or role of wage differentials); 2 marks for application (1 mark for each correct percentage); up to 2 marks for analysis of the narrowing differential and its effect on labour supply.'),
('t3-data-02b', null, null, 'Knowledge: Determinants of labour supply to an occupation: wages, non-monetary factors (working conditions, career prospects), alternative job opportunities, migration. Demand for labour is derived demand.
Application: Vacancy rate peaking at 10.6% in 2022; pay close to the legal minimum; unsocial hours and few promotion opportunities; supermarkets paying more; ageing population; tighter visa rules.
Analysis: Low supply of labour: low pay relative to alternative jobs and poor non-monetary benefits mean many potential workers prefer other occupations; high turnover (24-30%) means constant recruitment. Rising demand: the ageing population increases the derived demand for care workers, but providers cannot raise wages because council fees are constrained, so the market cannot clear at the prevailing wage, leaving vacancies.
Evaluation: Vacancies fell from 10.6% to 7.0% as overseas recruitment increased and pay rose, suggesting supply-side factors are important and responsive. The 2022 peak may reflect temporary post-pandemic labour shortages across the economy. Funding constraints may be the root cause, as they prevent wages rising to clear the market. Relative importance of pay versus conditions is difficult to judge.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 5 marks; Evaluation up to 3 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-2 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (3-5 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (6-8 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t3-data-02c', null, null, 'Knowledge: Minimum wage as a price floor in the labour market; competitive and monopsony labour market models.
Application: Councils as dominant purchasers of care and providers as large local employers (monopsony characteristics); providers'' claims that they cannot afford higher pay; rising demand for care.
Analysis: Competitive model: a minimum wage above equilibrium reduces the quantity of labour demanded and increases supply, creating unemployment (excess supply) in the sector (diagram). Monopsony model: if employers have wage-setting power, a minimum wage set between the monopsony wage and the competitive wage makes the MCL horizontal up to the supply curve, so employment can rise as well as wages (diagram). Higher pay would also reduce vacancies and turnover by attracting workers.
Evaluation: The outcome depends on the degree of monopsony power and the level at which the wage is set. Demand for care labour is relatively inelastic (care needs are essential and staffing ratios are regulated), so job losses may be small. But if councils do not increase fees, providers may cut hours or close, reducing employment. Lower turnover may reduce recruitment and training costs, offsetting part of the wage increase.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 6 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-3 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (4-6 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (7-10 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t3-data-02d', null, null, 'Knowledge: Government intervention in labour markets: minimum wages, public-sector pay setting, subsidies and funding, training, migration policy; labour market flexibility and mobility.
Application: Options in Extract B: sector minimum wage, national pay scale with career progression, training funding; tighter visa rules; ageing population; council funding constraints.
Analysis: Sector minimum wage: raises pay relative to alternatives, increasing labour supply (movement along supply) and reducing turnover. National pay scale with career progression: improves non-monetary and long-term rewards, shifting labour supply right. Training: increases occupational mobility and productivity, raising MRP. Relaxing visa rules: shifts labour supply right quickly. Additional central funding to councils: allows fees and wages to rise without provider closures.
Evaluation: Cost and opportunity cost: higher wages need extra public funding, which may require higher taxes or cuts elsewhere. Time lags: training takes time; migration is quickest but politically contested and may depress wages. A minimum wage without funding could cause closures. Regional variation in labour markets suggests a national scale may be too inflexible. Technology may reduce labour needs. Combination of pay and conditions likely needed. Justified judgement on the most effective policy.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 9 marks; Evaluation up to 6 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-5 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (6-10 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (11-15 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t3-essay-01', null, null, 'Knowledge: Characteristics of oligopoly: high concentration, interdependence, barriers to entry, product differentiation. Collusive and non-collusive behaviour; game theory; kinked demand curve; price and non-price competition; efficiency concepts.
Application: Examples such as supermarkets, mobile phone networks, energy supply, airlines and banking; cases of cartels being fined by competition authorities.
Analysis: Against consumers: collusion (formal or tacit) allows firms to act like a joint monopoly, setting price above MC, causing allocative inefficiency and loss of consumer surplus; barriers to entry protect supernormal profits; price rigidity (kinked demand curve) may keep prices high; heavy spending on advertising and branding raises costs. In favour: price wars benefit consumers; supernormal profits fund research and development (dynamic efficiency); economies of scale allow lower costs and prices than a competitive market; non-price competition improves quality and choice.
Evaluation: Depends on whether firms collude or compete: game theory shows incentives to cheat on collusion. Contestability: low barriers can keep prices close to costs. Effectiveness of competition policy and regulation. Different markets behave differently (e.g. supermarkets highly competitive on price, others less so). Short-run versus long-run effects. Judgement on overall impact.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, often in the form of definitions; little application to examples or the economy; analysis is descriptive or confused; evaluation, if attempted, is unsupported assertion.
Level 2 (7-12 marks): Accurate knowledge with some relevant application; chains of reasoning are developed for one or two points but are partial or one-sided; diagrams may be present but are not fully explained; evaluation is limited and generic.
Level 3 (13-18 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to relevant, realistic examples; several developed chains of reasoning with appropriate diagrams; evaluation is present across the answer, considering alternative outcomes, but is not consistently supported or prioritised.
Level 4 (19-25 marks): Precise, wide-ranging knowledge applied throughout to well-chosen examples; fully developed, logical chains of reasoning with accurate, integrated diagrams; sustained evaluation that weighs arguments, prioritises the most significant factors and reaches a fully justified overall judgement.'),
('t3-essay-02', null, null, 'Knowledge: Natural monopoly; regulation of privatised utilities; RPI - X and RPI + K price caps; performance targets; regulatory capture; asymmetric information.
Application: Ofwat, Ofgem and other regulators; consumer bills; water leakage and sewage discharge targets; investment needs in infrastructure.
Analysis: Price caps (RPI - X) limit price rises to inflation minus an efficiency factor, protecting consumers from monopoly pricing while giving firms an incentive to cut costs (they keep any extra savings until the next review). The K factor allows funding for investment. Performance targets ensure firms do not cut costs by reducing quality, with financial penalties for failure.
Evaluation: Asymmetric information: regulators depend on firms for cost data, making it hard to set the right X. Regulatory capture can lead to lenient caps. Firms may cut investment or quality to meet caps and still pay high dividends, especially if financed by heavy borrowing. Setting targets and monitoring them is costly; penalties may be passed on to consumers. Alternatives include nationalisation, profit (rate of return) regulation or increasing competition. Judgement on overall effectiveness, possibly differing between sectors.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
