Level 4 (19-25 marks): Precise, wide-ranging knowledge applied throughout to well-chosen examples; fully developed, logical chains of reasoning with accurate, integrated diagrams; sustained evaluation that weighs arguments, prioritises the most significant factors and reaches a fully justified overall judgement.'),
('t3-essay-03', null, null, 'Knowledge: Profit maximisation (MC = MR); alternative objectives: revenue maximisation (MR = 0), sales maximisation (AC = AR), satisficing, survival, growth, corporate social responsibility. Divorce of ownership from control; principal-agent problem.
Application: Public limited companies with dispersed shareholders; owner-managed small businesses; social enterprises and not-for-profit organisations; tech firms prioritising growth over short-term profit; firms emphasising ethical sourcing.
Analysis: Profit maximisation is assumed in traditional theory because profits reward owners, fund investment and are needed for survival in competitive markets; shareholders can replace managers who fail to deliver. But managers may pursue their own objectives (salary linked to size, prestige), leading to revenue or growth maximisation; satisficing occurs where stakeholders must be kept satisfied. Diagram showing different output levels for each objective.
Evaluation: Objectives vary by type of firm, stage of life cycle and market structure: firms in highly competitive markets have little choice but to aim for profit; new firms may focus on survival or growth. Long-run profit maximisation may require sacrificing short-run profit (e.g. limit pricing, market share). Firms often lack the information on MC and MR to profit maximise precisely. Performance-related pay and share options can align managers with shareholders. Overall judgement.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, often in the form of definitions; little application to examples or the economy; analysis is descriptive or confused; evaluation, if attempted, is unsupported assertion.
Level 2 (7-12 marks): Accurate knowledge with some relevant application; chains of reasoning are developed for one or two points but are partial or one-sided; diagrams may be present but are not fully explained; evaluation is limited and generic.
Level 3 (13-18 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to relevant, realistic examples; several developed chains of reasoning with appropriate diagrams; evaluation is present across the answer, considering alternative outcomes, but is not consistently supported or prioritised.
Level 4 (19-25 marks): Precise, wide-ranging knowledge applied throughout to well-chosen examples; fully developed, logical chains of reasoning with accurate, integrated diagrams; sustained evaluation that weighs arguments, prioritises the most significant factors and reaches a fully justified overall judgement.'),
('t4-mcq-01', 'C', 'Terms of trade = (index of export prices / index of import prices) x 100 = 110 / 125 x 100 = 88.0. The terms of trade have deteriorated: each unit of exports now buys fewer imports. 113.6 inverts the formula.', null),
('t4-mcq-02', 'B', 'Opportunity cost of 1 tonne of wheat: X = 2 metres of cloth, Y = 1.5 metres, so Y has the comparative advantage in wheat. Opportunity cost of 1 metre of cloth: X = 0.5 tonnes of wheat, Y = 0.67 tonnes, so X has the comparative advantage in cloth. Both can gain from specialisation and trade despite X''s absolute advantage.', null),
('t4-mcq-03', 'D', 'Both free trade areas and customs unions remove tariffs between members, but only a customs union also sets a common external tariff on goods from outside. A single currency is a feature of monetary union and free labour movement is a feature of a common market.', null),
('t4-mcq-04', 'A', 'Tariff revenue = tariff per unit x quantity imported after the tariff = £2 x 300,000 = £600,000. Revenue is not earned on the 600,000 tonnes imported before the tariff.', null),
('t4-mcq-05', 'B', 'Building or buying productive assets abroad is outward foreign direct investment, recorded in the financial account. Future profits remitted to the UK from the factory would later appear as primary income credits.', null),
('t4-mcq-06', 'A', 'The dollar price rises from $250 (£200 x 1.25) to $280 (£200 x 1.40), an increase of $30 / $250 = 12%. An appreciation of sterling makes UK exports more expensive in foreign currency.', null),
('t4-mcq-07', 'C', 'The Marshall-Lerner condition states that a depreciation improves the current account when the combined PED of exports and imports exceeds 1. Because demand is often inelastic in the short run, the balance may first worsen before improving (the J-curve effect).', null),
('t4-mcq-08', 'C', 'Lower relative unit labour costs mean UK firms can produce at lower cost per unit than foreign rivals, improving price competitiveness. Slower productivity growth than wages raises unit labour costs, and an appreciation makes UK goods dearer abroad.', null),
('t4-mcq-09', 'D', 'The usual UK measure of relative poverty is household income (adjusted for household size) below 60% of the median. The first option describes absolute poverty.', null),
('t4-mcq-10', 'B', 'Gini = area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve / total area under the line of equality = 0.15 / (0.15 + 0.35) = 0.30. A value of 0.43 wrongly divides by the area under the Lorenz curve only.', null),
('t4-mcq-11', 'A', 'The HDI combines three dimensions: a long and healthy life (life expectancy), knowledge (mean and expected years of schooling) and a decent standard of living (GNI per capita at PPP).', null),
('t4-mcq-12', 'D', 'Inelastic demand and supply mean shifts caused by weather, discoveries or global demand produce large price swings, so earnings and government revenue fluctuate, discouraging investment and planning. Primary products typically have a low, not high, income elasticity of demand.', null),
('t4-mcq-13', 'B', 'As lender of last resort, the central bank lends to banks that are fundamentally solvent but short of liquidity, preventing a bank run from spreading through the financial system.', null),
('t4-mcq-14', 'C', 'Tax = 20% x £38,000 + 40% x £10,000 = £7,600 + £4,000 = £11,600. Average rate = £11,600 / £60,000 = 19.3%. The marginal rate on the last pound earned is 40%.', null),
('t4-mcq-15', 'A', 'The cyclical deficit results from the operation of automatic stabilisers during a downturn and should disappear when the economy recovers. A deficit that persists at trend output is structural, and the accumulated stock of borrowing is the national debt.', null),
('t4-short-01', null, null, 'Knowledge: In a floating system the exchange rate is determined by the demand for and supply of the currency. Depreciation: a fall in the value of one currency in terms of another.
Application: Applied to sterling and the dollar: Bank of England and US Federal Reserve interest rates; UK current account position; investor confidence.
Analysis: Lower UK interest rates relative to US rates: investors move ''hot money'' to dollar assets, reducing demand for sterling and increasing its supply, so the pound depreciates. A worsening UK current account (e.g. higher imports): UK residents supply more pounds to buy foreign currency, while demand for pounds for UK exports falls. Other valid factors: speculation, political uncertainty reducing confidence, quantitative easing, higher UK inflation relative to the US.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: Up to 2 marks for each factor: 1 mark for identifying a relevant factor and 1 mark for explaining how it changes the demand for or supply of sterling.'),
('t4-short-02', null, null, 'Knowledge: Absolute poverty: income insufficient to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clothing. Relative poverty: income well below the typical level in society (in the UK, below 60% of median household income).
Application: Use of the UK 60% of median threshold or an international extreme poverty line; example of growth concentrated in high-skilled sectors.
Analysis: Relative poverty depends on the distribution of income, not just its level. If growth raises the incomes of middle and high earners faster than those at the bottom (for example, gains from rising returns to skills or capital), median income rises and the relative poverty line rises with it, so more households fall below it even if their real incomes have not fallen. Absolute poverty may fall at the same time.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: Up to 2 marks for knowledge (1 for each definition); 1 mark for application; up to 2 marks for analysis explaining why growth can increase relative poverty.'),
('t4-short-03', null, null, 'Knowledge: Lender of last resort: the central bank provides emergency liquidity to banks that cannot obtain funds elsewhere. Moral hazard: when protection from risk encourages riskier behaviour.
Application: Applied to a bank facing a run by depositors or a sudden loss of wholesale funding; the Bank of England.
Analysis: Banks lend long but borrow short, so even a solvent bank can run out of cash if depositors withdraw funds suddenly. By lending to it, the central bank prevents a bank failure from spreading to other banks and the wider economy (systemic risk). However, if banks expect to be rescued, they may take excessive risks, knowing that losses will be limited (moral hazard), making future crises more likely.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge of the lender-of-last-resort role; 1 mark for application; up to 2 marks for analysis (1 for why the role prevents wider failure, 1 for a developed explanation of the problem, e.g. moral hazard).'),
('t4-data-01a', null, null, 'Knowledge: Terms of trade = (index of average export prices / index of average import prices) x 100.
Application: 2015: 112 / 94 x 100 = 119.1. 2025: 92 / 115 x 100 = 80.0. The terms of trade fell by about 33% between 2015 and 2025.
Analysis: A deterioration means Kanara must export more cocoa and copper to buy the same quantity of imports such as machinery and fuel, reducing the real purchasing power of export earnings. This limits the imports of capital goods needed for development and contributes to the fall in government revenue. It is consistent with the view that primary product prices tend to fall relative to manufactured goods over time.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge of the formula; 2 marks for application (1 mark each for 119.1 and 80.0); up to 2 marks for analysis of the significance of the deterioration.'),
('t4-data-01b', null, null, 'Knowledge: Factors influencing growth and development: primary product dependency, volatility of commodity prices, the Prebisch-Singer hypothesis, savings gap, access to credit, infrastructure, governance.
Application: Cocoa and copper rising from 68% to 77% of export earnings; terms of trade falling; government revenue drop in 2024 when copper prices fell; low cocoa yields; few local jobs in mining.
Analysis: Price volatility: inelastic demand and supply mean commodity prices swing sharply; export earnings and tax revenue become unpredictable, leading to cuts in spending on schools and roads that damage human capital and infrastructure. Declining terms of trade (Prebisch-Singer): low income elasticity of demand for primary products means their prices tend to fall relative to manufactures, limiting the gains from trade. Limited linkages: mining is capital-intensive and foreign-owned, so profits may be repatriated and few local jobs are created.
Evaluation: GDP per capita and HDI have still risen and poverty has fallen, so dependence has not prevented growth. Commodity booms can provide revenue for investment if managed well (e.g. via a sovereign wealth fund). The key constraint may be governance and corruption rather than commodity dependence itself. Price swings can be positive as well as negative.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 5 marks; Evaluation up to 3 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-2 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (3-5 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (6-8 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t4-data-01c', null, null, 'Knowledge: Foreign direct investment (FDI): investment by a multinational company in productive assets in another country. Development versus growth. Impacts of multinationals on host countries.
Application: Net FDI rising from US$0.6 billion to US$2.1 billion; investment by two mining multinationals after royalty cuts; few local workers employed; imported machinery; 30% of population without electricity.
Analysis: Positive: FDI fills the savings gap, increases the capital stock and output, raises export volumes, and may bring technology, skills and infrastructure (e.g. roads, power) that benefit others. AD rises through investment and multiplier effects. Tax revenue can fund public services. Negative: lower royalties reduce the government''s share of mining income; profits are repatriated (primary income outflows); few jobs and weak linkages to the local economy; environmental damage; increased dependence on copper.
Evaluation: The impact depends on the terms negotiated: royalty cuts mean much of the value may leave the country. HDI rose only from 0.55 to 0.56 between 2020 and 2025 despite rising FDI, suggesting limited development benefits so far. Corruption in licence allocation may divert benefits. Time lags: skills and infrastructure benefits take time. Overall judgement.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 6 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-3 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (4-6 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (7-10 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t4-data-01d', null, null, 'Knowledge: Market-orientated strategies: trade liberalisation, privatisation, promotion of FDI. Interventionist strategies: development of human capital, infrastructure, managed use of resource revenues, support for diversification and processing. Measures of development.
Application: Finance ministry plan: tariff cuts, privatising electricity, tax breaks for FDI. Alternative plan: sovereign wealth fund, rural infrastructure and education, cocoa processing co-operatives. 30% without electricity; low cocoa yields; volatile copper revenues.
Analysis: Market-orientated: tariff cuts lower the cost of imported inputs and increase competition; privatisation may improve efficiency and attract investment into electricity supply; FDI brings capital and technology. Interventionist: a sovereign wealth fund smooths revenue and protects public spending from commodity price falls; education and infrastructure raise productivity and shift LRAS right; processing cocoa locally adds value, diversifies exports and creates jobs, reducing dependence on raw commodities.
Evaluation: Market strategies may increase inequality, and privatised electricity may not extend supply to unprofitable rural areas; tax breaks reduce revenue needed for public services; tariff cuts may harm infant industries. Interventionist strategies require effective, non-corrupt government and funding, and have long time lags; processed chocolate may face tariff escalation in export markets. Corruption is a constraint on both. A combination may be best, e.g. FDI for capital alongside public investment in education. Justified judgement.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 9 marks; Evaluation up to 6 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-5 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (6-10 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (11-15 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t4-data-02a', null, null, 'Knowledge: The budget deficit is a flow: the amount by which government spending exceeds revenue in a year. The national debt is a stock: the accumulated total of past borrowing.
Application: The deficit fell from 13.8% to 4.6% of GDP, but net debt rose from 94% to 97% of GDP.
Analysis: As long as the government runs a deficit, it must borrow and add to the stock of debt, so a smaller deficit only slows the growth of debt rather than reducing it. The debt-to-GDP ratio rises when debt grows faster than nominal GDP; with a deficit of around 4.6% of GDP and nominal GDP growing more slowly than debt, the ratio continues to climb. A surplus, or nominal GDP growth faster than debt, would be needed for the ratio to fall.
Evaluation: Not required for this question; no marks are awarded for evaluation.
Mark allocation: 1 mark for knowledge (deficit as a flow and debt as a stock); up to 2 marks for application using Table 1; up to 2 marks for analysis of why a falling deficit still increases debt and how the ratio depends on nominal GDP growth.'),
('t4-data-02b', null, null, 'Knowledge: Cyclical deficit (automatic stabilisers) and structural/discretionary changes in fiscal policy.
Application: Deficit rising from 2.6% to 13.8% of GDP; tax revenues falling as output contracted; spending on job-retention schemes, business support and health.
Analysis: Automatic stabilisers: the sharp fall in output reduced incomes, profits and spending, so income tax, corporation tax and VAT receipts fell, while welfare spending rose automatically, increasing the cyclical deficit. Discretionary spending: the government chose to spend heavily on job-retention and business support schemes to protect jobs and firms, and on health services, sharply increasing spending. The fall in GDP itself also raises the deficit as a percentage of GDP.
Evaluation: Discretionary spending was probably the larger factor given the scale of support schemes. Much of the increase was temporary: the deficit fell back to 4.9% by 2022-23, suggesting it was largely cyclical and one-off. However, it remained above its 2019-20 level, implying some structural deterioration. Very low bond yields (0.5%) made borrowing cheap at the time.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 5 marks; Evaluation up to 3 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-2 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (3-5 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (6-8 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t4-data-02c', null, null, 'Knowledge: Debt interest as a component of current public expenditure; opportunity cost; bond yields; fiscal space; distribution effects.
Application: Debt interest rising from £27 billion (2020-21) to £102-108 billion; bond yields rising from 0.5% to 4.3%; index-linked bonds; interest costs exceeding several departmental budgets.
Analysis: Opportunity cost: money spent on interest cannot be used for health, education or investment, or for tax cuts, reducing fiscal space and possibly long-run growth. Higher interest bills raise the deficit, adding further to debt and creating a risk of a debt spiral if yields keep rising. Higher government bond yields can raise borrowing costs throughout the economy, crowding out private investment. Interest paid to overseas holders of debt is a primary income outflow in the current account.
Evaluation: As a share of GDP the increase may be less dramatic than the cash figures suggest, since nominal GDP also grew with inflation. Part of the rise was due to temporary high inflation affecting index-linked bonds, so it may fall back. Much interest is paid to domestic holders (including pension funds), so it is partly a transfer within the economy. Significance depends on whether yields stay high and whether growth outpaces interest rates.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 6 marks; Evaluation up to 4 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-3 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (4-6 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (7-10 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t4-data-02d', null, null, 'Knowledge: Fiscal policy and fiscal rules; austerity; the debt-to-GDP ratio; crowding out; credit ratings and bond yields; the role of growth and interest rates in debt dynamics.
Application: Debt at 97% of GDP; bond yields at 4.3%; debt interest over £100 billion; proposed threshold freezes, investment cuts and pay restraint; economists'' warnings in Extract B.
Analysis: Case for prioritising: reduces debt interest costs and opportunity cost; rebuilds fiscal space to respond to future shocks; maintains investor confidence and avoids sudden rises in bond yields; reduces the burden on future generations; lower government borrowing may reduce crowding out. Case against: tax rises and spending cuts reduce AD, slowing growth and raising unemployment (fiscal multiplier), which can make the debt ratio harder to reduce; cutting public investment damages long-run productive capacity; freezing thresholds increases tax burdens through fiscal drag, hitting lower earners.
Evaluation: Debt dynamics depend on the gap between interest rates and nominal growth: growth-friendly policies may reduce the ratio more effectively than austerity. The state of the economy matters: consolidation is less damaging when there is a positive output gap. The composition of measures matters: cutting current rather than capital spending protects growth. International comparisons show some countries sustain higher debt ratios. The credibility of fiscal rules matters for bond markets. Justified judgement, e.g. a gradual, growth-focused path rather than rapid consolidation.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 9 marks; Evaluation up to 6 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-5 marks): Isolated or partially accurate knowledge; little or no application to the context or data; analysis is absent or consists of unconnected assertions; any evaluation is generic and unsupported.
Level 2 (6-10 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to the context with some use of data; analysis develops relevant chains of reasoning but they may be incomplete or one-sided, and diagrams may contain minor errors; evaluation is present but limited, asserted rather than supported.
Level 3 (11-15 marks): Precise, relevant knowledge applied consistently to the context and data; analysis develops clear, logical and well-linked chains of reasoning, supported by accurate, integrated diagrams where relevant; evaluation is balanced, supported by evidence and leads to a justified judgement.'),
('t4-essay-01', null, null, 'Knowledge: Tariffs as a tax on imports; reasons for protectionism (infant industries, strategic industries, jobs, dumping, balance of payments); tariff diagram; comparative advantage and the gains from trade; WTO rules.
Application: UK steel industry facing competition from lower-cost producers and global overcapacity; steel''s role in defence, construction and the transition to low-carbon technologies; communities dependent on steelworks.
Analysis: Tariff diagram: domestic price rises from world price to world price plus tariff; domestic production increases, imports fall, government gains tariff revenue; consumer surplus falls and there are deadweight losses from inefficient production and lower consumption. Arguments for: protects jobs in concentrated communities, avoiding structural unemployment; protects a strategic industry for national security; counters dumping of subsidised steel; supports the domestic supply of steel for infrastructure.
Evaluation: Higher steel prices raise costs for UK manufacturers and construction, reducing their competitiveness and possibly costing more jobs than are saved. Risk of retaliation against UK exports and conflict with WTO commitments. Protection may reduce incentives for UK producers to become efficient (dynamic inefficiency) and may simply delay decline. Alternatives such as subsidies for decarbonisation, anti-dumping duties targeted at specific countries, or retraining may be better. Judgement may differ between short-run anti-dumping measures and permanent protection.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
Levels:
Level 1 (1-6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, often in the form of definitions; little application to examples or the economy; analysis is descriptive or confused; evaluation, if attempted, is unsupported assertion.
Level 2 (7-12 marks): Accurate knowledge with some relevant application; chains of reasoning are developed for one or two points but are partial or one-sided; diagrams may be present but are not fully explained; evaluation is limited and generic.
Level 3 (13-18 marks): Accurate knowledge applied to relevant, realistic examples; several developed chains of reasoning with appropriate diagrams; evaluation is present across the answer, considering alternative outcomes, but is not consistently supported or prioritised.
Level 4 (19-25 marks): Precise, wide-ranging knowledge applied throughout to well-chosen examples; fully developed, logical chains of reasoning with accurate, integrated diagrams; sustained evaluation that weighs arguments, prioritises the most significant factors and reaches a fully justified overall judgement.'),
('t4-essay-02', null, null, 'Knowledge: Inequality of income and wealth; Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient; progressive, proportional and regressive taxes; means-tested and universal benefits; causes of inequality.
Application: UK income tax bands and higher rates; Universal Credit, child benefit and state pensions; the effect of taxes and benefits on the UK Gini coefficient for original versus disposable income.
Analysis: Progressive taxes take a larger proportion of income from higher earners, compressing the distribution of post-tax income. Means-tested benefits raise the incomes of the poorest households. Together they shift the Lorenz curve towards the line of equality and reduce the Gini coefficient for disposable income compared with original income. Revenue can also fund public services used more by low-income households.
Evaluation: Disincentive effects: high marginal tax rates may discourage work, enterprise and investment (Laffer curve); the poverty trap from benefit withdrawal reduces incentives to increase hours. Tax avoidance and evasion by high earners and the mobility of capital limit effectiveness. Indirect taxes such as VAT are regressive, offsetting progressive income tax. Wealth inequality is largely untouched by income taxes. Policies treat symptoms rather than causes (skills, labour market power); education and minimum wages may be more effective in the long run. Overall judgement.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
