Level 4 (19-25 marks): Precise, wide-ranging knowledge applied throughout to well-chosen examples; fully developed, logical chains of reasoning with accurate, integrated diagrams; sustained evaluation that weighs arguments, prioritises the most significant factors and reaches a fully justified overall judgement.'),
('t4-essay-03', null, null, 'Knowledge: Market failure in the financial sector: asymmetric information, externalities (systemic risk), moral hazard, speculation and market bubbles, market rigging. Regulation: capital and liquidity requirements, stress tests, ring-fencing, macroprudential policy; role of the central bank and regulators.
Application: The 2008 global financial crisis and bank rescues; Bank of England''s Financial Policy Committee and Prudential Regulation Authority; ring-fencing of retail banking; stress testing.
Analysis: Higher capital requirements mean banks can absorb losses without failing; liquidity requirements reduce the risk of bank runs; ring-fencing protects retail deposits from riskier investment banking; stress tests identify vulnerabilities early. Regulation addresses moral hazard created by implicit government guarantees and reduces negative externalities from bank failures on the wider economy.
Evaluation: Regulation may push risky activity into less regulated ''shadow banking''. Higher capital requirements may reduce lending and slow growth. Regulators face information gaps and risk regulatory capture; crises often arise in unexpected places. Global finance means national regulation needs international coordination. Other measures matter: credible central bank lender-of-last-resort facilities, macroprudential tools (e.g. limits on mortgage lending), deposit insurance, and monetary policy avoiding asset bubbles. Judgement: regulation necessary but not sufficient on its own.
Mark split: Knowledge, Application and Analysis up to 16 marks; Evaluation up to 9 marks.
